      "page_title": "Deadlocks: Detect, Cause, Cure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. InnoDB Gap Locks",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- idx has 10, 20, 30",
            "SELECT * FROM t",
            "  WHERE id BETWEEN 12 AND 25",
            "  FOR UPDATE;  -- locks (10,20]",
            "-- and gap (20,30): no INSERT 22"
          ],
          "note": "next-key = record + gap before it"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT id FROM jobs",
            "WHERE state='ready'",
            "ORDER BY id LIMIT 1",
            "FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;",
            "-- NOWAIT: error 55P03 instead"
          ],
          "note": "SKIP LOCKED = lock-free job queue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Minimal Example",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- T1            -- T2",
            "UPDATE acct A;  UPDATE acct B;",
            "UPDATE acct B;  UPDATE acct A;",
            "-- T1 waits B   -- T2 waits A"
          ],
          "note": "opposite order = cycle"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Detection Mechanism",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Engine builds the __wait-for graph__ of blocked transactions",
            "Finds a cycle, picks a **victim**, rolls it back",
            "Postgres: checks only after ==deadlock_timeout== (1s default)",
            "InnoDB: detects near-instantly on lock request",
            "Victim = least work done / fewest undo records"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "Signal",
            "Code"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Postgres",
              "deadlock detected",
              "40P01"
            ],
            [
              "MySQL",
              "Deadlock found...",
              "1213"
            ],
            [
              "MySQL wait",
              "lock wait timeout",
              "1205"
            ],
            [
              "SQL Server",
              "chosen as victim",
              "1205"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Cure: Order, Shrink, Retry",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- always low id first",
            "UPDATE acct WHERE id = LEAST(a,b);",
            "UPDATE acct WHERE id = GREATEST(a,b);",
            "-- sort IDs before the loop too"
          ],
          "note": "consistent order breaks the cycle"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Sort** the rows: same order everywhere, no cycle possible",
            "**Shrink**: no HTTP call, message publish or user think-time inside",
            "Index the WHERE — a __table scan__ locks every row it reads",
            "Timeouts (`lock_timeout`, `innodb_lock_wait_timeout`) cap *waits*, not cycles"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Cycles in the wait-for graph"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Deadlock Triage & Serializable's Two Faces",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Deadlock vs Wait vs Livelock",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Symptom",
            "Cause",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Deadlock",
              "cycle",
              "order + retry"
            ],
            [
              "Lock wait",
              "one hot row",
              "shorten txn"
            ],
            [
              "Livelock",
              "all retry at once",
              "jittered backoff"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Key rule",
          "text": "Deadlocks are a **retry** case, not a crash: catch 40P01 / 1213, ROLLBACK, sleep with jitter, replay the whole transaction. Page 8 does the same for 40001."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Reading the evidence",
          "items": [
            "MySQL: `SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS`",
            "section 'LATEST DETECTED DEADLOCK'",
            "Postgres: server log deadlock DETAIL",
            "SQL Server: XML deadlock graph (XE)"
          ],
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Treating a deadlock as a fatal bug — it is **expected** under concurrency",
            "Retrying only the failed statement, not the whole transaction",
            "Raising `deadlock_timeout` to 'fix' it — hides the wait, keeps the cycle",
            "Retrying instantly with no jitter — the same two collide again"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Two Ways to Be Serializable",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "__SERIALIZABLE__ is a promise about the ==result==, not about the mechanism. Two engines keep it two opposite ways.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Style",
            "How",
            "Failure mode"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Locking (strict 2PL)",
              "range/next-key locks",
              "blocks, deadlock"
            ],
            [
              "SSI (optimistic)",
              "tracks rw-deps",
              "**aborts at COMMIT**"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "ERROR:  could not serialize",
            "  access due to read/write",
            "  dependencies among transactions",
            "SQLSTATE 40001"
          ],
          "note": "arrives at COMMIT, not at the SELECT"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : cache `bal` from attempt 1 and reuse it in attempt 2 and the ==write skew== comes straight back — you paid for SERIALIZABLE and got nothing.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "SSI, retries, engines"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "SSI Internals & the 40001 Retry Loop",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Mechanism: rw-Dependencies",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Postgres takes __SIREAD__ predicate locks on what you read",
            "T1 reads what T2 later writes = one ==rw-edge==",
            "Two rw-edges in a cycle = **dangerous structure**",
            "Engine kills one transaction, does not wait"
          ],
          "style": "number"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Example: The Retry Loop",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "for n in range(5):",
            "  try:",
            "    with db.begin():",
            "      bal = q(\"SELECT ...\")  # re-read!",
            "      q(\"INSERT ...\", bal)",
            "    break",
            "  except Serialization:",
            "    sleep(0.05 * 2**n * random())"
          ],
          "note": "replay whole transaction, reads included"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Engine Comparison",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "Level",
            "Reality"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{icon:postgresql} PG",
              "SERIALIZABLE",
              "SSI, 40001 aborts"
            ],
            [
              "{icon:mysql} MySQL",
              "SERIALIZABLE",
              "adds `LOCK IN SHARE MODE`"
            ],
            [
              "SQL Server",
              "SNAPSHOT",
              "optimistic, update conflict"
            ],
            [
              "SQL Server",
              "SERIALIZABLE",
              "**range locks**, blocking"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Price of SSI",
          "items": [
            "Predicate-lock bookkeeping per read",
            "**False positives**: aborts on safe pairs",
            "Abort rate climbs fast with contention",
            "Long transactions hold SIREAD locks"
          ],
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`try/catch` that logs 40001 instead of retrying",
            "Retrying forever — no attempt cap, no jitter",
            "Reusing values read in the failed attempt",
            "SERIALIZABLE everywhere when one query needed it"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Two strategies for the __lost update__ of page 3: ==lock first== (pessimistic) or ==check at write time== (optimistic).",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "SSI, retries, engines"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Locking vs Versioning: The Two Patterns",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` — one hot row",
            "__UNIQUE constraint__ — no duplicate inserts",
            "`UPDATE t SET b=b-100 WHERE b>=100`",
            "SERIALIZABLE only for **write-skew** rules"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The rule",
          "text": "SERIALIZABLE without a retry loop is weaker than READ COMMITTED — it just fails louder. Earn it on invariants: balances, bookings, on-call rosters."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Pessimistic: lock first",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SELECT qty FROM stock",
            "  WHERE id=7 FOR UPDATE;  -- X lock",
            "UPDATE stock SET qty=qty-1",
            "  WHERE id=7;",
            "COMMIT;  -- lock released here"
          ],
          "note": "lock held for whole transaction"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Optimistic: verify at write",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- read: qty=5, version=12",
            "UPDATE stock SET qty=4,",
            "  version=13",
            "WHERE id=7 AND version=12;",
            "-- rowcount 0 = someone won,",
            "-- re-read and retry"
          ],
          "note": "rowcount is the whole check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Same two users, both ways",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Pessimistic",
            "Optimistic"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A reads",
              "locks row",
              "v=12, free"
            ],
            [
              "B reads",
              "**blocks**",
              "v=12, free"
            ],
            [
              "A writes",
              "qty 5→4",
              "v→13, 1 row"
            ],
            [
              "B writes",
              "sees 4, →3",
              "**0 rows**, retry"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Compare-and-set: no read",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "UPDATE accounts",
            "SET balance = balance - 100",
            "WHERE id=1 AND balance>=100;",
            "-- 0 rows = insufficient funds"
          ],
          "note": "one statement, no race window"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "Few hot rows, everyone fighting → ==pessimistic==. Many rows, conflicts rare → ==optimistic==. Retry cost above lock cost flips the choice."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Defaults Per Engine",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Nobody ships the same __default isolation level__. Your code is portable; its ==concurrency behaviour is not==.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Two ways to win a race"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Trade-offs, ORM Traps & Advisory Locks",
      "blocks": [
